WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.974 --> 00:00:01.807
- When you think of Wrigley,

00:00:01.807 --> 00:00:02.858
the first thing that come to your mind

00:00:02.858 --> 00:00:05.109
is the grass, the marquee out front,

00:00:05.109 --> 00:00:06.405
and the scoreboard.

00:00:06.405 --> 00:00:09.363
It's like one of the main
body parts of Wrigley field.

00:00:09.363 --> 00:00:10.287
I'm Darryl Wilson.

00:00:10.287 --> 00:00:13.572
I'm a scoreboard operator
for Chicago Cubs.

00:00:13.572 --> 00:00:16.072
(jazzy music)

00:00:19.514 --> 00:00:22.188
I've been working at
Wrigley field since 1987.

00:00:22.188 --> 00:00:23.021
Wrigley field is one of the oldest

00:00:23.021 --> 00:00:24.872
ballparks besides Boston.

00:00:24.872 --> 00:00:26.829
It's been around for over a hundred years

00:00:26.829 --> 00:00:28.451
and basically nothing has changed

00:00:28.451 --> 00:00:31.607
on the scoreboard since 1938.

00:00:31.607 --> 00:00:33.207
In the scoreboard, we
have at least three guys,

00:00:33.207 --> 00:00:35.115
if we're lucky four.

00:00:35.115 --> 00:00:36.201
While the Cubs game is going on

00:00:36.201 --> 00:00:39.336
my job is to post scores inning by inning

00:00:39.336 --> 00:00:42.919
and pitching changes
throughout the league.

00:00:44.304 --> 00:00:47.054
(crowd cheering)

00:00:49.273 --> 00:00:50.388
Once all the games
start and the scoreboard,

00:00:50.388 --> 00:00:52.241
it's pretty chaotic.

00:00:52.241 --> 00:00:53.663
You're writing scores
down on your clipboard

00:00:53.663 --> 00:00:54.937
and you're changing scores while

00:00:54.937 --> 00:00:57.002
you're also yelling at other guys

00:00:57.002 --> 00:00:59.538
to change their scores in their games.

00:00:59.538 --> 00:01:00.494
I can say though it's hot.

00:01:00.494 --> 00:01:01.819
It's very noisy.

00:01:01.819 --> 00:01:03.748
The walkways are a little narrow.

00:01:03.748 --> 00:01:06.262
You got plates, numbers everywhere.

00:01:06.262 --> 00:01:07.251
The switch out of the scoreboard plates

00:01:07.251 --> 00:01:09.452
starts with a shift punch just

00:01:09.452 --> 00:01:11.274
to pop it out of the slots.

00:01:11.274 --> 00:01:14.136
The plates are made out
of a lighter thin metal.

00:01:14.136 --> 00:01:14.969
You definitely have to latch

00:01:14.969 --> 00:01:16.179
if you wanna make sure that they're locked

00:01:16.179 --> 00:01:17.905
in, so that if the wind's blowing out

00:01:17.905 --> 00:01:20.484
they won't blow out at you
or something like that.

00:01:20.484 --> 00:01:21.600
You're running up and down

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.452
and changing scores for
eight different games.

00:01:24.452 --> 00:01:28.368
I would say it's definitely hard work.

00:01:28.368 --> 00:01:29.387
The view is outstanding.

00:01:29.387 --> 00:01:31.368
It's just like a 360 degree view

00:01:31.368 --> 00:01:35.272
of the entire ballpark
and the neighborhood.

00:01:35.272 --> 00:01:37.031
It's like looking out to your backyard

00:01:37.031 --> 00:01:38.284
and having a perfect lawn.

00:01:38.284 --> 00:01:39.179
I mean, that's what it feel like.

00:01:39.179 --> 00:01:41.587
You're walking out, you just feel proud.

00:01:41.587 --> 00:01:42.800
You're just proud of your work

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:46.207
and the work that the guys put into it.

00:01:46.207 --> 00:01:47.430
The reason why I believe
that the scoreboard

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:49.400
is special to fans, because it still

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:53.904
keeps that old luster of
the old time ballpark.

00:01:53.904 --> 00:01:54.737
I've seen a lot of modern parks,

00:01:54.737 --> 00:01:57.663
but Wrigley field has an
authentic original feel

00:01:57.663 --> 00:01:59.580
of how baseball began.

